fact WorkItem(id: "WI-852", description: some(value: "CLI parse errors render a BYTE OFFSET, not `path:line:col` — the span-aware `format_with_source` twin exists and exactly one binary uses it.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED (2026-07-28, HEAD 47a0a730 + WI-850). Driving `anthill check` on a file whose only fault is a converter diagnostic prints, with exit 1:\n  error: /tmp/.../defaulted.anthill: parse error at 86..95: operation `foo`: type parameter `T` carries a default …\nThe byte pair is `ParseError`'s `Display` (parse/error.rs:28-36). The span-aware twin `ParseError::format_with_source` (parse/error.rs:22-25) renders `line:col: message` and IS in the tree — used by `anthill-todo` (main.rs:654) and two tests, and by nothing else. Every CLI parse-error site formats with `Display`: anthill-cli/src/main.rs:555-558 (load / check / query / codegen cpp), :853-856 (`codegen rust`), and :1417-1422 + :1474-1477, which print `parse error: {e}` without even naming the FILE.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. WI-745 gave LOAD errors a `path:line:col` rendering (`LoadError::Located`, whose whole point was that a raw byte offset \"named nothing once N files merged into one KB\"); PARSE errors were left behind. So which STAGE found the fault decides whether the author gets a clickable location or a byte offset they have to count to — a distinction the author cannot predict, and one that widens as converter-stage refusals multiply: WI-805, WI-808, WI-809 and WI-850 are all reported as parse errors.\n\nWORK. Give parse errors the same file + `line:col` rendering load errors have. The source text is in hand at every one of those call sites (it was just read from disk), so this is a RENDERING change, not a plumbing one. Decide where it belongs: a `Located`-style wrapper mirroring WI-745, so ONE owner renders both error families and they cannot drift, or a fix at each of the four call sites — prefer the wrapper, and state the reason if not. The two sites printing `parse error: {e}` must also name the file. Check `anthill run` / `anthill-todo` / the bundle path for further parse-error printers before assuming four is the whole list — enumerating printers is exactly where WI-805 went wrong.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `anthill check <file>` on a source carrying a converter diagnostic prints `path:line:col: message`; a test in anthill-cli pins the RENDERED STRING (a bad fixture plus an assertion on the message, not just on the exit code); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found 2026-07-28 while delivering WI-850, driving that ticket's new refusal through the real CLI to confirm it was located. It named the file and the operation but pointed at bytes 86..95.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-cli/src/main.rs, rustland/anthill-core/src/parse/error.rs."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T20:56:54Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-852", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. Parse errors now render `path:line:col: message`, the rendering WI-745 gave load errors.\n\nTHE OWNER. `span::render_located(path, body, has_span)` owns the file-prefix rule; `LoadError::located_render` delegates to it and the new `ParseError::format_located(&Path, &str)` calls it.\n\nWHY NOT THE CARRIER THE TICKET PREFERRED — the reason is written at `format_located`, as the ticket required. The two families TRAVEL differently. A `LoadError` is raised deep in the loader with no `&ParsedFile` in hand and reaches `load_all`'s caller merged with N files' errors, so its file identity must RIDE with it — that is what WI-745's `Located` is for. A `ParseError` is returned by `parse()` straight to the caller that just read the text, and is rendered in that same expression, so a carrier would be constructed and consumed on one line. The one place that DID hold `ParseError`s across a boundary — `PersistenceError::Parse` — now renders at its raise site, so nothing in the tree stores an unrendered `ParseError`.\n\nThe cost of a rendering (a call-site obligation instead of a type invariant) is paid off differently: `ParseError`'s sourceless `Display` AND its `std::error::Error` impl are DELETED, not documented as discouraged. Measured before deleting — nothing else in the workspace referenced them; every remaining consumer uses `{e:?}` / `expect` (Debug). A printer that forgets to pass the source no longer compiles.\n\nNINE PRINTERS, not the four the ticket named — and enumerating was the risk it flagged (WI-805). anthill-cli `main.rs` (the shared file-parse loop behind load/check/query/codegen-cpp; `codegen rust`; both `collect_queries` arms), `run.rs`, anthill-stl `stdlib.rs`, anthill-todo bundle + project files, anthill-core persistence, and the anthill-rust-gen GENERATED bundle template — a printer living inside a string literal, verified by running the ignored `emitted_bundle_compiles` test.\n\nSYNTHESIZED SOURCES. `collect_queries` parses `--import` lines + the user's text, so a raw span indexes into text the author only partly wrote. Split at the prefix: a span past it is SHIFTED back into the user's text, so `--query-file` reports the same line whether or not `-i` was passed (unshifted it drifted one line per flag — a location that is worse than none); a span inside the prefix names `--import`; an inline `--pattern` names the flag, because a line:col there would point into the import lines and the synthesized `fact` keyword, i.e. at text the author never wrote.\n\nTESTS. `anthill-cli/tests/wi852_parse_error_location_test.rs`, 7 tests pinning RENDERED STRINGS, including `stage_does_not_change_the_rendering` (one fixture through check / load / run / codegen rust must yield the IDENTICAL location) and a whole-stderr negative control on the byte-offset format. Verified by stashing the source changes and re-running: 7/7 FAIL against pre-change code, 7/7 pass after. Full workspace green, 3596 passed / 0 failed.\n\nREVIEW (/simplify, 4 agents) — applied: dropped `Option<&Path>` from `format_located` (no caller passed None, and my doc had named two None users that do not exist — the WI-809 fabricated-justification shape again); collapsed `PersistenceError::Parse` to `Vec<String>` rendered at the raise site (kills a measured 32 -> 72 byte widening of `Result<(), PersistenceError>` on per-fact persist/retract paths, plus a whole-file copy); `render_located` takes `body` by value so the pathless arm moves; `format_with_source` now uses the previously-DEAD `Span::format_start`; `QueryOrigin` enum -> `Option<(&Path, &str)>`; the import prefix is built in one place so the two arms cannot disagree about the split point.\n\nNOT DONE, deliberately, all outside this ticket's scope:\n  - Four near-identical read -> parse -> locate loops (cli main.rs x2, run.rs, todo) and three embedded-source parse loops (stdlib, todo bundle, generated template) stay separate; consolidating them changes control flow in three commands.\n  - `Span::line_col` walks from byte 0 per error, so N errors on one file is O(N x len). A reviewer measured it: a corrupted 3.1 MB workitems.anthill with 688 errors spends ~1.3 s debug / ~0.10 s release in location rendering. It is an error path that then exits; a shared line-start index would also pay off for load.rs's ~20 hand-rolled line:col arms.\n  - The two `collect_queries` LOAD printers (scan_definitions errors) stay unlocated, so inside that one function the stage still decides. Stamping `.with_path()` there would be WRONG while the source is a concatenation: the line would be shifted and then falsely attributed to the file. It is blocked on the `--import` defect filed separately.", at: "2026-07-28T20:56:48Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-852", author: "claude", content: "/code-review OUTCOME — 13 findings; 6 fixed, 7 recorded. Full workspace 3598 passed / 0 failed. The one that mattered was DRIVEN before I acted on it, and it was a live defect I had shipped.\n\nFINDING 1, CONFIRMED BY DRIVING — my own fix reintroduced the defect this ticket removes. `located_query_parse_error` split the synthesized query source on `span.start < prefix_len`. But when the user's text does not begin a valid top-level item, tree-sitter's recovery merges the `--import` prefix and that text into ONE `ERROR` node starting at byte 0. Measured, on a query file beginning `) oops`:\n  without -i:  error: <path>:1:1: syntax error near `) oops`\n  with -i:     error: --import: syntax error near `import wi852.good ) oops`\n— the file is never named, the location is gone, and the author is pointed at a flag that is not at fault. The existing test could not catch it: `bad-query.anthill` starts with `fact`, a valid top-level start, so recovery does not merge.\nFIX: attribute by OVERLAP, not by where the span STARTS. A span lying WHOLLY inside the prefix is an `--import` fault; a span reaching past it is located in the user's text with a start that began in the prefix CLAMPED to the user's first byte. New test `a_span_merged_across_the_prefix_is_located_in_the_file` with a `) oops` fixture; verified it FAILS against the `start`-keyed rule and passes after.\n\nFINDING 2 exposed the SAME false attribution one level down. The review proposed naming which `-i` flag by line index. Implemented, then driven — and the index was WRONG: with `-i wi852.good -i 'not a name!!'` the recovery node covers BOTH import lines, so the flag at its start is `wi852.good`, blaming the first flag for the second's fault. Naming a range instead: `--import (one of `a`, `b`): …` when a span covers several, `--import `a`: …` when it covers one. Never wrong, and strictly more than the bare `--import:` the second diagnostic (\"unexpected top-level node: ERROR\") otherwise carried alone. The lesson generalises: a synthesized-source span identifies a REGION, and every attribution from it must be a region too.\n\nFINDING 3 — I wrote an overclaiming doc again, the WI-809 shape. `render_located`'s note said each family's `line:col` half \"is spelled by `Span::format_start`\", and load.rs said the two families \"render identically, by construction\". Both false: `format_start` has ONE caller (`ParseError::format_with_source`) and `LoadError` hand-rolls `Span::line_col` + `format!` in ~20 arms. The docs now state exactly what is enforced — the PREFIX is shared; the position half is two implementations agreeing by CONVENTION and able to drift silently. Routing load.rs's 20 arms through `format_start` would make the stronger claim true and is the real fix; it is outside this ticket.\n\nFINDING 11 + 12, tests. `prepended_import_lines_do_not_shift_the_file_location` was the only one of the seven asserting neither an exit code nor a control — fixed. And `assert_no_byte_offsets` had become UNFALSIFIABLE: it anchored on the literal \"parse error at\", which no code path can emit any more since `impl Display for ParseError` was deleted, so it guarded only against re-adding that exact wording and would miss any other sourceless rendering. Replaced with a POSITIVE control — every `error:` line naming the fixture must carry `:<digits>:<digits>:` — which fails the moment a diagnostic names the file without saying where in it.\n\nRECORDED, NOT FIXED:\n  4. `render_located(path, body, has_span)` is boolean-blind — nothing checks that `has_span` agrees with how the caller built `body`. Two callers, both correct, and the /simplify reviewer judged the 3-arg shape right-depth; deriving the flag would mean restructuring both families' body construction.\n  5. The generated-bundle printer is compile-checked only by the `#[ignore]`d `emitted_bundle_compiles`. Run manually after every edit to it here (passes); un-ignoring it puts a nested `cargo check` of anthill-core in the default suite.\n  6. Deleting `Display` + `impl Error` on `ParseError`, and re-typing `PersistenceError::Parse` to `Vec<String>`, IS a breaking change for a downstream consumer doing `parse::parse(s)?` into `Box<dyn Error>` or inspecting a parse error's span programmatically. anthill-core is not published (the bundle generator still describes crates.io as future), and the enforcement — a printer that forgets the source does not compile — is what the carrier design would otherwise have had to buy. Deliberate, with that cost stated.\n  7. `Span::line_col` walks from byte 0 per error (O(N x len)). Measured by a reviewer at ~1.3 s debug / ~0.10 s release for 688 errors on a 3.1 MB file. Error path that then exits; the fix (one line-start index per source) belongs with load.rs's 20 arms.\n  8. The `--import`/shift mechanism remains a bandaid over the WI-853 root cause, which the WI-853 ticket says to DELETE once imports are supplied structurally. Kept because the flag is documented and the machinery is now correct; WI-853 carries the instruction to remove it.\n  9. The span shift clones the `ParseError` to overwrite two u32s. Kept: building the body at the call site instead would re-spell `format_with_source`'s format, which is the drift this ticket exists to prevent.\n  13. Seven near-identical read -> parse -> locate loops stay separate — as recorded in the delivery note.", at: "2026-07-29T05:47:19Z")
